{"name":"Navajo","hex":"#efdcc3","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Navajo","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A member of the Navajo people, currently the largest Native American tribe in North America."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["An Apachean (Southern Athabaskan) language of the Athabascan language family belonging to the Na-Dené phylum. It is spoken by 149,000 people in the American Southwest (Arizona, New Mexico, Utah, and Colorado).","An Amerindian people who traditionally speak the Navajo language."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Navajo"}],"entries":[{"title":"Navajo","blocks":[{"text":"The Navajo are an Indigenous People of the Southwestern United States. The Navajo term for themselves is Diné. Their language is Navajo, a Southern Athabaskan language. The states with the largest Diné populations are Arizona (140,263) and New Mexico (108,305). More than three-quarters of the Diné population resides in these two states."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Manuelito.jpg/330px-Manuelito.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Navajo","wikidata":"Q108266"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/19th_century_knowledge_indian_lore_navaho_house.jpg/500px-19th_century_knowledge_indian_lore_navaho_house.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/19th_century_knowledge_indian_lore_navaho_house.jpg/500px-19th_century_knowledge_indian_lore_navaho_house.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A 19th-century hogan","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A19th_century_knowledge_indian_lore_navaho_house.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c6/Navaho_spinning_and_weaving_page_928.png?utm_source=en.wiki